    {
      "id": "library/nist-sp-800-207/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "History of Zero Trust Efforts Related to Federal Agencies",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-11-history-of-zero-trust-efforts-related-to-fede.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The concept of zero trust has been present in cybersecurity since before the term “zero trust” was \ncoined. The Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) and the Department of Defense \npublished their work on a more secure enterprise strategy dubbed “black core” [BCORE]. Black \ncore involved moving from a perimeter-based security model to one that focused on the security \nof individual transactions. The work of the Jericho Forum in 2004 publicized the idea of de-\nperimeterization—limiting implicit trust based on network location and the limitations of relying \non single, static defenses over a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/nist-sp-800-207/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Structure of This Document",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-12-structure-of-this-document.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The rest of the document is organized as follows: \n• Section 2 defines ZT and ZTA and lists some assumptions when designing a ZTA for an \nenterprise. This section also includes a list of the tenets of ZT design. \n• Section 3 documents the logical components, or building blocks, of ZT. It is possible that \nunique implementations compose ZTA components differently yet serve the same logical \nfunctionality. \n \n1 https://go.forrester.com/blogs/next-generation-access-and-zero-trust/  \n2 Any mention of commercial products or services within NIST documents is for information only; it does not imply a",
      "truncated": true,
